function heardBody(): string {
  return `
  <div class="article-hero-vis">
    <div class="article-hero-image" aria-hidden="true" style="background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(13,4,16,.15) 40%, rgba(13,4,16,.65) 100%), url('/images/generated/blog-eye-of-the-heart.jpg')">
      <span class="ahi-badge">${iconSvg('flask')} Third Eye Research · Applied · Ongoing</span>
    </div>
    <p class="article-hero-caption">Evidence review accompanying HEARD's field study of 137 participants — an ongoing, applied research programme at Third Eye Worldwide.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="article-body-wrap">
    <aside class="article-toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <div class="toc-label">On this page</div>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#s1" class="active">Abstract</a></li>
        <li><a href="#s2">Key findings</a></li>
        <li><a href="#s3">Loneliness as a public health crisis</a></li>
        <li><a href="#s4">Why being heard helps</a></li>
        <li><a href="#s5">The stranger effect</a></li>
        <li><a href="#s6">Lay & peer listeners</a></li>
        <li><a href="#s7">The populations HEARD serves</a></li>
        <li><a href="#s8">The AI companion ceiling</a></li>
        <li><a href="#s9">Gaps & limitations</a></li>
        <li><a href="#s10">Recommendations</a></li>
        <li><a href="#s11">References</a></li>
      </ol>
    </aside>

    <div class="article-body">
      <p class="deck">A large and convergent body of evidence in psychology, neuroscience, and public health supports the central thesis behind HEARD: much of what is labeled "depression" in isolated people is, in part, a starvation for connection and for being genuinely received by another mind — and the act of being heard by a real human is a measurable, physiological intervention, not just a comfort.</p>

      <p>The healing ingredient is not advice or clinical expertise but <em>felt understanding</em>. Naming emotions calms the brain's threat system, disclosure to a safe listener lowers shame, and lay, peer, and befriending listeners with no clinical license produce real reductions in loneliness and depressive symptoms. AI companions can blunt loneliness briefly but hit a documented ceiling, because people value empathy less once they know it comes from a machine.</p>

      <p>This paper reviews that evidence and reads it against HEARD's own field study of 137 participants — an ongoing, applied research programme. It is deliberately honest about where the science is strong and where it is thin.</p>

      <h2 id="s1">Abstract</h2>
      <p>HEARD proposes that remote, voice-based listening — delivered by listeners who are themselves often isolated (blind and low-vision people, isolated elders, refugees in bureaucratic limbo, disabled people) — is both a loneliness intervention and a form of dignified paid work. The model is doubly powerful because the same populations most starved for connection are also well suited to be the listeners. The science says this can work. The honest gaps are around dose, durability, safeguarding, and the absence of trials of paid lay-listening as a standalone loneliness intervention.</p>

      <blockquote class="pull-quote">
        The claim is narrow and well supported: in chronically isolated people, a large share of the distress coded as "depression" is a response to the absence of being witnessed — and reintroducing genuine, attentive human listening relieves a meaningful portion of it.
        <cite>— The thesis, stated plainly</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <h2 id="s2">Key findings</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Loneliness is a public health emergency with mortality on the scale of smoking.</strong> The 2023 US Surgeon General's advisory frames social disconnection as carrying mortality risk comparable to smoking up to 15 cigarettes a day.</li>
        <li><strong>Loneliness (subjective) and isolation (objective) are distinct</strong> — and the subjective feeling is the stronger driver of depression. What changes loneliness is feeling understood.</li>
        <li><strong>The brain treats being heard as a physiological event.</strong> Affect labeling dampens amygdala activity; feeling understood lights up reward circuitry; supportive presence lowers the brain's threat response.</li>
        <li><strong>People often disclose more honestly to strangers</strong> than to family. Distance and the absence of social consequences can make honesty safe.</li>
        <li><strong>Lay and peer listeners work.</strong> Trained non-clinicians delivering simple talking support produce measurable improvements in depression and loneliness.</li>
        <li><strong>HEARD's acute populations are among the loneliest — and can both heal and be employed by the model.</strong> Blind people may also be uniquely good listeners.</li>
        <li><strong>AI companions help a little, then plateau.</strong> The active ingredient seems to be knowing a real conscious mind is receiving you, which AI cannot supply.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="s3">Loneliness and isolation as a public health crisis</h2>
      <p>In May 2023 the US Surgeon General, Dr. Vivek Murthy, issued an 81-page advisory, "Our Epidemic of Loneliness and Isolation," declaring loneliness a public health crisis. Its headline framing is that the mortality impact of social disconnection is similar to smoking up to 15 cigarettes a day, and greater than the risk associated with obesity and physical inactivity. Murthy described loneliness as "like hunger or thirst" — a signal the body sends when something needed for survival is missing.</p>

      <div class="stat-row" role="list">
        <div class="sr-cell" role="listitem">
          <div class="n"><em>+50%</em></div>
          <div class="l">increased likelihood of survival for people with stronger social relationships (Holt-Lunstad et al., 2010; 148 studies, 308,849 participants)</div>
        </div>
        <div class="sr-cell" role="listitem">
          <div class="n"><em>~50%</em></div>
          <div class="l">of US adults had experienced loneliness even before the pandemic, per the 2023 Surgeon General's advisory</div>
        </div>
        <div class="sr-cell" role="listitem">
          <div class="n"><em>+26%</em></div>
          <div class="l">increased risk of premature death associated with lacking social connection</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>Crucially, loneliness and social isolation are not the same thing. Social isolation is the objective absence of contacts; loneliness is the subjective, distressing perception that one's relationships are inadequate. You can be surrounded by people and lonely, or alone and content — and it is the subjective experience of loneliness that most strongly and independently predicts depression. This is the empirical hinge of the HEARD thesis: the cure is not merely more people in the room, it is the felt experience of being understood by one of them.</p>

      <h2 id="s4">Why talking and being heard actually helps</h2>
      <h3>Affect labeling: naming the feeling calms the brain</h3>
      <p>Matthew Lieberman and colleagues at UCLA (2007, <em>Psychological Science</em>, "Putting Feelings Into Words") showed with fMRI that when people put feelings into words, activity in the amygdala — the brain's threat and alarm center — decreased, while activity rose in the right ventrolateral prefrontal cortex. Saying "I feel abandoned" or "I'm terrified" out loud is not venting for its own sake; it measurably turns down the body's alarm.</p>

      <h3>Feeling understood is rewarding to the brain</h3>
      <p>Morelli, Torre, and Eisenberger (2014, <em>SCAN</em>) found that feeling understood activated reward and social-connection regions, while <em>not</em> feeling understood activated regions tied to negative affect. Harry Reis's work places "felt understanding" at the center of well-being — and it improves outcomes even between strangers, raising interaction satisfaction and reducing perceived pain. Direct evidence that a listener need not be a loved one to deliver the benefit.</p>

      <h3>Social baseline theory: another person lowers the cost of threat</h3>
      <p>James Coan's social baseline theory holds that the human brain treats relationships as bioenergetic resources that reduce the effort of regulating threat. In his hand-holding fMRI studies (Coan, Schaefer &amp; Davidson, 2006), holding a partner's hand reduced threat-related brain activity; holding a stranger's hand helped less, but still more than being alone. A listener is literally a load-sharing resource for a distressed nervous system.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        ${iconSvg('warning-circle', 'fill')}
        <div>
          <strong>The active ingredient</strong>
          <p>It is not the words of comfort but the knowledge that a real conscious mind chose to receive you. AI can simulate listening; it cannot witness you. That is precisely what HEARD supplies and AI structurally cannot.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 id="s5">The stranger effect: why family is sometimes too close to be safe</h2>
      <p>A recurring, counterintuitive finding is that people often disclose more honestly to strangers than to intimates. Zick Rubin's "stranger on the train" phenomenon (1975) describes how people reveal private information to those they will never see again — the logic is the absence of long-term consequences. Sandstrom and Dunn (2014) and Epley and Schroeder (2014) showed that people systematically underestimate how good talking to strangers will feel.</p>

      <p>This connects to the ancient, cross-cultural practice of confession. The structural common denominator across the priest in the booth, the bartender, the barber, and the warmline volunteer is a safe, semi-anonymous, low-consequence listener. For HEARD's populations this is decisive: a refugee may not be able to tell their family about despair without endangering relationships; an elderly person may not want to burden adult children; a blind listener who has never seen the speaker may be the single safest confidant available. Family is sometimes too close to be safe.</p>

      <h2 id="s6">Lay, peer, and befriending listeners: does non-clinical talking work?</h2>
      <p>Yes, with nuance. The strongest single piece of evidence is the Friendship Bench in Zimbabwe. In a cluster randomized trial (Chibanda et al., 2016, <em>JAMA</em>), trained lay health workers delivering problem-solving talk therapy on a park bench produced large improvements: at 6 months the intervention group had far lower rates of depression symptoms than controls.</p>

      <div class="stat-row" role="list">
        <div class="sr-cell" role="listitem">
          <div class="n"><em>13.7%</em></div>
          <div class="l">depression-symptom rate in the Friendship Bench intervention group at 6 months...</div>
        </div>
        <div class="sr-cell" role="listitem">
          <div class="n"><em>49.9%</em></div>
          <div class="l">...versus the control group (adjusted risk ratio 0.28; Chibanda et al., 2016)</div>
        </div>
        <div class="sr-cell" role="listitem">
          <div class="n"><em>~500K</em></div>
          <div class="l">calls, texts, and chats connected by the California Peer-Run Warm Line since July 2019</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>Vikram Patel's broader program of work repeatedly shows that trained non-specialists can deliver effective psychosocial care — a strategy called <strong>task-shifting</strong>. Warmlines (peer-staffed, non-crisis listening lines) are the closest real-world analog to HEARD: users show reduced use of crisis services and increased community integration over time. The pattern that emerges: one-to-one, synchronous, genuinely interactive contact with a purpose helps most; passive or purely logistical contact helps least.</p>

      <h2 id="s7">The acute populations: starved for connection, and well suited to listen</h2>
      <h3>Blind and low-vision people</h3>
      <p>Vision loss is one of the conditions most strongly linked to loneliness, anxiety, and depression. In a Dutch study, 54% of people 55 and older with visual impairment scored as lonely, versus 44% of sighted peers. The flip side is the <strong>heightened-listening hypothesis</strong>: a robust neuroscience literature documents that early blindness produces real auditory advantages through cross-modal neuroplasticity — superior voice discrimination and sharper sound localization (Fine et al., <em>Journal of Neuroscience</em>, 2019). A blind listener may be neurologically primed to attend to the voice, and a sighted speaker, knowing they are unseen, may feel uniquely unjudged.</p>

      <div class="figure">
        <div class="figure-frame">
          <span class="fig-label">Fig. 01</span>
          ${iconSvg('chats-teardrop', 'regular', 'fig-ico')}
        </div>
        <div class="figure-cap">
          <span class="fc-num">Fig. 01</span>
          <span>The "eye of the heart": safe non-judgment (the stranger effect plus literal non-observation) combined with heightened auditory attention is a plausible mechanism for why blind listeners could be exceptional at this work. No direct trial yet tests it — this is where the 137-person field study compares against the literature.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Refugees and asylum seekers in limbo</h3>
      <p>Forced migrants carry very high rates of mental illness, and prolonged bureaucratic waiting is itself a documented cause of harm. A meta-analysis (Côté-Olijnyk et al., 2024) found that among asylum seekers in indefinite waiting, roughly 49.5% experienced depression, 43.0% anxiety disorders, and 40.8% PTSD. For this group, a trained listener outside the family and community gossip network is a uniquely safe confidant — and paid remote listening restores the "meaningful activity" whose absence the reviews identify as a core harm.</p>

      <h3>Isolated elderly people</h3>
      <p>Loneliness among older adults is widespread (a pooled prevalence of 28.5% of adults 60 and over) and a strong, independent predictor of depression and dementia. Here the evidence for talking-based help is strongest: an updated meta-analysis (2023, <em>Journal of Affective Disorders</em>) of 42 studies found a large effect of life-review and reminiscence therapy on late-life depression. Older people often carry an urgent need to tell their story and be witnessed before it is lost — and being listened to is the therapy.</p>

      <h2 id="s8">The AI companion ceiling</h2>
      <p>AI companions are a real and growing phenomenon, and the honest finding is "promise with a ceiling." The Harvard Business School working paper by De Freitas et al. (2024) found that AI companions alleviate loneliness "on par only with interacting with another person." Tellingly, Replika markets itself as "here to make you feel HEARD, because it genuinely cares about you."</p>

      <p>But a two-year Aalto University study (Yuan et al., CHI 2026) found companion use coinciding with increased signals of loneliness and depression over time. The deepest evidence concerns <em>why</em>: a study of over 6,000 participants (Rubin, Perry et al., 2025, <em>Nature Human Behaviour</em>) found identical empathic messages rated as more supportive, more resonant, and more caring when believed to come from a human — and people were willing to wait days for a human reply rather than get an instant AI one.</p>

      <blockquote class="pull-quote">
        AI can simulate listening; it cannot witness you.
        <cite>— On the AI companion ceiling</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <h2 id="s9">Gaps and limitations</h2>
      <p>This review is deliberately read next to the 137-person field study. The honest gaps:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No randomized trial of paid lay-listening as a standalone loneliness intervention exists yet.</strong> The evidence is assembled from adjacent fields — the field study is filling a genuine gap, not duplicating one.</li>
        <li><strong>"Every single participant improved" is a strong claim and should be framed carefully.</strong> A 100% response rate likely reflects an immediate, self-reported mood lift rather than durable remission. Reporting both the immediate effect and follow-up would strengthen it.</li>
        <li><strong>Befriending and warmline evidence is thinner and more observational</strong> than clinical-trial evidence; much of it is pre-post and uncontrolled.</li>
        <li><strong>The "blind people are uniquely good listeners" claim is an inference, not a tested finding.</strong> The auditory-neuroplasticity evidence is solid; the leap to better listening outcomes is not yet directly demonstrated.</li>
        <li><strong>Safeguarding and dose are unresolved.</strong> Disclosure can transiently increase distress before it helps; a listening platform needs clear boundaries and suicide-risk escalation pathways.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="s10">Recommendations</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Frame the claim precisely and let it ride on the strongest science</strong> — felt understanding, affect labeling, and the loneliness-mortality data.</li>
        <li><strong>Use the stranger effect as a feature, not an apology.</strong> Market the safety of a listener outside your family and community as a designed advantage.</li>
        <li><strong>Instrument the platform like a study.</strong> Adopt validated short measures (UCLA Loneliness 3-item, PHQ-2/9) at first contact and follow-up, benchmarking against the Friendship Bench.</li>
        <li><strong>Stage the employment model around safeguarding.</strong> Train listeners, pay fairly, supervise, and build clear crisis-escalation pathways.</li>
        <li><strong>Run a small controlled comparison when feasible</strong> — even a modest waitlist-controlled design would lift HEARD from promising to evidence-based.</li>
        <li><strong>Test the blind-listener hypothesis explicitly.</strong> Compare speaker-rated felt understanding across blind and sighted listeners.</li>
      </ol>

      <hr/>

      <p>The science and the mission point the same way. Loneliness is lethal at population scale, the subjective feeling of not being understood drives depression more than mere aloneness, and the brain treats being listened to as a real physiological event. People will tell a safe stranger what they cannot tell their family, and trained non-clinicians can deliver real relief. AI can imitate all of this and still falls short for the reason that turns out to be the whole point: people need to know a conscious being is actually receiving them. The thesis that being heard by a real human is, for isolated people, much of the cure is not sentimental. It is, on the present evidence, largely correct.</p>

      <div class="footnotes">
        <h4 id="s11">References &amp; notes</h4>
        <ol>
          <li>US Surgeon General (2023). <em>Our Epidemic of Loneliness and Isolation.</em> Office of the Surgeon General advisory.</li>
          <li>Holt-Lunstad, J., Smith, T. B. &amp; Layton, J. B. (2010). Social relationships and mortality risk: a meta-analytic review. <em>PLoS Medicine.</em></li>
          <li>Lieberman, M. D. et al. (2007). Putting feelings into words. <em>Psychological Science.</em></li>
          <li>Morelli, S. A., Torre, J. B. &amp; Eisenberger, N. I. (2014). The neural bases of feeling understood. <em>SCAN.</em></li>
          <li>Coan, J. A., Schaefer, H. S. &amp; Davidson, R. J. (2006). Lending a hand: social regulation of the neural response to threat. <em>Psychological Science.</em></li>
          <li>Chibanda, D. et al. (2016). Effect of a primary care-based psychological intervention (Friendship Bench). <em>JAMA.</em></li>
          <li>Fine, I. et al. (2019). Auditory advantages in early blindness. <em>Journal of Neuroscience.</em></li>
          <li>Côté-Olijnyk, M. et al. (2024). Mental health of asylum seekers in prolonged waiting. Meta-analysis.</li>
          <li>De Freitas, J. et al. (2024). AI Companions Reduce Loneliness. <em>HBS Working Paper 24-078.</em></li>
          <li>Rubin, M., Perry, A. et al. (2025). Comparing the value of perceived human versus AI-generated empathy. <em>Nature Human Behaviour.</em></li>
          <li>TEWW HEARD Field Study, 137 participants — ongoing applied research. Method appendix in the research library.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  `;
}

function thirdEyeKitBody(): string {
  return `
  <div class="article-hero-vis">
    <div class="article-hero-image" aria-hidden="true" style="background-image: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(13,4,16,.15) 40%, rgba(13,4,16,.65) 100%), url('/images/generated/blog-third-eye-kit.jpg')">
      <span class="ahi-badge">${iconSvg('flask')} Open Hardware · Field-Tested · Herat, Afghanistan</span>
    </div>
    <p class="article-hero-caption">Built and field-tested by team ABRT01 at the Herat Blind Support Centre; presented at TEKNOFEST's Barrier-Free Living category.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="article-body-wrap">
    <aside class="article-toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <div class="toc-label">On this page</div>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#tek1" class="active">Abstract</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tek2">The Infrastructure Gradient</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tek3">Design philosophy</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tek4">Four independent nodes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tek5">The real-time isolation boundary</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tek6">The fail-silent baseline</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tek7">Consent architecture</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tek8">The validation debt ledger</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tek9">Recognition &amp; field record</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tek10">References</a></li>
      </ol>
    </aside>

    <div class="article-body">
      <p class="deck">Third Eye Kit is a body-distributed sensing system that gives a blind or low-vision wearer real-time, non-visual awareness of obstacles above and below their line of travel, and gives their family a way to locate them — over cellular infrastructure that may be as thin as a single bar of 2G signal. It was designed, built, and field-tested against a problem most assistive-technology roadmaps quietly assume away: the Infrastructure Gradient.</p>

      <p>A refreshable Braille display retails from roughly $3,500 to $15,000 — a price point that is not a product, it is an exclusion. Third Eye Kit targets a $50–100 manufacturing cost using commodity, regionally-sourceable components, and treats intermittent power, unmapped terrain, high ambient noise, and economic barriers as first-class design constraints rather than edge cases.</p>

      <h2 id="tek1">Abstract</h2>
      <p>Team ABRT01 — high-school students at Afgan-Türk Maarif Okulları (Herat Erkek Lisesi), advised by the school's mechatronics department — built Third Eye Kit as an entry in TEKNOFEST's Barrier-Free Living category. Rather than a lab demonstrator, it was tested with real users at the Herat Blind Support Centre: multi-session obstacle-course trials, feedback on alert distances and feedback modality, and wearability assessment. The project's own technical spec is explicit about what is proven and what is not — every capability is tagged Validated For or Not Validated For, and the roadmap is read as a promise, not a disclaimer.</p>

      <div class="stat-row" role="list">
        <div class="sr-cell" role="listitem">
          <div class="n"><em>$50–100</em></div>
          <div class="l">target manufacturing cost at scale, versus $3,500–$15,000 for a refreshable Braille display</div>
        </div>
        <div class="sr-cell" role="listitem">
          <div class="n"><em>4</em></div>
          <div class="l">independent microcontroller nodes, each with its own sensing job and execution loop</div>
        </div>
        <div class="sr-cell" role="listitem">
          <div class="n"><em>≈$117</em></div>
          <div class="l">landed prototype cost at current, non-scale component pricing</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 id="tek2">The Infrastructure Gradient</h2>
      <p>The "Infrastructure Gradient" is the compounding set of environmental deficits that make first-world assistive hardware inapplicable in the environments that need it most:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Intermittent power</strong> — no assumption of nightly charging or stable grid supply; the system is designed to degrade predictably as voltage sags rather than fail erratically. Power budget: 4–6 hours per node on commodity alkaline cells.</li>
        <li><strong>Unmapped terrain</strong> — no dependence on pre-surveyed maps, indoor beacons, or curated navigation data. Ranging is done live and locally via ultrasonic time-of-flight, with no cellular triangulation or assisted-GPS dependency.</li>
        <li><strong>High ambient noise</strong> — open-air markets and busy streets defeat audio-only feedback, so alerts are redundant across haptic and local-audio channels; no single channel is a point of failure.</li>
        <li><strong>Economic barriers</strong> — commercial assistive hardware routinely prices out the people who need it most.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="tek3">Design philosophy</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Frugal engineering.</strong> Prefer a $5 commodity sensor with a known failure mode over a $500 integrated module with an opaque one — buildability and reparability in-region outrank feature count.</li>
        <li><strong>Hardware–software isolation.</strong> Subsystems with hard real-time deadlines are physically separated from subsystems with soft, multi-second deadlines, so latency cannot leak across a boundary that shares no processor.</li>
        <li><strong>Context-bounded design.</strong> The system declares, in writing, exactly the envelope it is validated inside — and refuses to imply guarantees outside it.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="tek4">Four independent nodes</h2>
      <p>Third Eye Kit is a four-node decentralized system. Each node is an independent microcontroller with its own sensing responsibility, its own feedback actuators, and its own execution loop:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Node A — Upper ranging (glasses).</strong> Detects head/torso-height hazards via ultrasonic sensing; feeds back through local audio. <em>Implemented.</em></li>
        <li><strong>Node B — Lower ranging (shoes, left + right).</strong> Detects ground-plane hazards below the knee; feeds back through haptic vibration plus a passive, ambient-light-triggered LED for after-dark visibility. <em>Implemented.</em></li>
        <li><strong>Node C — Manual/tactile (gloves).</strong> Near-field task support for objects within arm's reach (3–40 cm), with low-intensity haptic feedback. <em>In development.</em></li>
        <li><strong>Node D — Location telemetry (belt/pack).</strong> GPS fix plus 2G/GSM connectivity; replies to an authorized inbound request with the wearer's coordinates over SMS. <em>Implemented.</em></li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="tek5">The real-time isolation boundary</h2>
      <p>This is the architectural thesis of the project. An ultrasonic ranging cycle (Nodes A/B/C) is a microsecond-scale, timing-sensitive operation — distance in centimeters is the round-trip echo of a 40 kHz pulse divided by 58, and for a blind wearer, a silent node is a safety failure, not a glitch. A GSM location transaction (Node D) is the opposite: a multi-second, blocking sequence dictated by cellular towers, not firmware — registering on the network and waiting on a GPS fix can block for 30–90 seconds in poor conditions.</p>

      <blockquote class="pull-quote">
        These two workloads must never share a processor. A blocked cellular handshake cannot induce jitter, starvation, or a missed echo in the ranging domain.
        <cite>— The architectural thesis, Technical Specification §2.2</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p>Nodes A/B/C and Node D run on independent AVR microcontrollers with independent power and no shared clock, so isolation is structural — there is no scheduler misconfiguration or priority inversion to reason about, because the boundary is physical silicon, not a software guarantee.</p>

      <h2 id="tek6">The fail-silent baseline</h2>
      <div class="callout">
        ${iconSvg('warning-circle', 'fill')}
        <div>
          <strong>Known limitation, honestly flagged</strong>
          <p>If a sensor is disconnected, mis-wired, occluded, or supply voltage sags below operating range, the ranging loop returns a timeout or implausible value and produces no useful alert — the wearer gets no signal that the device has stopped protecting them. For a blind user, an unannounced failure is the most dangerous state the system can occupy. The project's own spec names this its top safety priority: a planned Fault Annunciation Layer would replace the fail-silent baseline with a deterministic fault state machine (<code>OK</code> / <code>SENSOR_FAULT</code> / <code>VOLTAGE_SAG</code> / <code>DEGRADED</code>), detected via hardware interrupts and a watchdog timer rather than polling, with distinct haptic signatures per fault and a ≤50 ms announcement budget. Status: <strong>planned, not yet implemented</strong> — the current build should not be relied on where an unannounced sensor failure would be unacceptable.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 id="tek7">Consent architecture</h2>
      <p>Location tracking of a vulnerable person is treated as a privacy-critical capability, not a convenience feature. Location sharing is meant to be user-held and revocable, and covert family-side activation is meant to be structurally blocked — no family member should be able to silently switch the wearer into a tracked state. The current v2 prototype responds to an inbound authorized request by texting back GPS coordinates, which is convenient but not yet a full consent gate. The roadmap adds a physical wearer-operated toggle, a firmware allowlist so only pre-authorized numbers can elicit a location reply, rate limiting against an authorized-but-abusive party, and no remote-provisioning path for adding a number without the wearer's own physical action.</p>

      <h2 id="tek8">The validation debt ledger</h2>
      <p>The single most important habit in this project's own documentation is refusing to imply guarantees it hasn't earned. "Validated" means demonstrated on v2 hardware in field conditions, including the Herat blind support centre; "Not Validated" means unverified or out of scope — not impossible, just not yet claimed.</p>
      <h3>Validated for</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Near-range detection of static, unstructured obstacles, upper and lower body</li>
        <li>Redundant haptic + local-audio alerting at a tunable threshold (~50–70 cm)</li>
        <li>Two-modality feedback surviving high ambient acoustic noise</li>
        <li>Standalone GPS fix + 2G/GSM location reporting with no broadband, Wi-Fi, or smartphone tether</li>
        <li>Passive after-dark visibility marker, automatic below an ambient-light threshold</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Not validated for</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Announced response to sensor or power failure (Fault Annunciation Layer is planned; baseline is fail-silent)</li>
        <li>A full user-held, revocable consent gate (planned; baseline answers inbound requests)</li>
        <li>Ingress protection against rain or dust (waterproofing in progress; no IP rating claimed)</li>
        <li>Long-term device-abandonment metrics, or battery aging under sustained extreme heat</li>
        <li>Dense, dynamic, or fast-moving obstacle fields; fall or medical-event detection; formal functional-safety certification</li>
        <li>Reflective, sound-absorbing, or acoustically transparent surfaces (a known limitation of ultrasonic sensing — soft fabrics, some glass)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Items move from "not validated for" to "validated for" only when there is hardware evidence — never because the roadmap sounds finished.</p>

      <h2 id="tek9">Recognition &amp; field record</h2>
      <p>Third Eye Kit was built by team ABRT01 (Afgan-Türk Maarif Okulları, Herat Erkek Lisesi) and presented at TEKNOFEST in the Barrier-Free Living category, and has separately been recognized with a Best Presentation Award at TEKNOFEST Black Sea. Both v1 and v2 prototypes were field-tested with blind and low-vision users at the Herat Blind Support Centre — multi-session obstacle-course trials with feedback on alert distances, feedback modality, and wearability. The project has also received local press coverage, including from Anadolu Agency and Bosnian outlet Faktor, both centered on the engineer's own story of vision loss during the Afghan war and the device he built in response.</p>

      <hr/>

      <p>Third Eye Kit's real contribution isn't any single sensor — it's the discipline of the Infrastructure Gradient as a design constraint, the physical isolation between microsecond-scale ranging and multi-second cellular telemetry, and a validation ledger that says exactly what has and hasn't been proven. That same "physics, not inference" and "context-bounded" ethos carries forward directly into the project's next chapter — see <a href="/blog-detail?slug=third-eye-kit-v2">Third Eye Kit v2</a>.</p>

      <div class="footnotes">
        <h4 id="tek10">References &amp; notes</h4>
        <ol>
          <li>Third Eye Kit — Technical Specification. <a href="https://github.com/sadeqisaidmohaddes-star/third-eye-kit/blob/main/docs/TECHNICAL_SPEC.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/sadeqisaidmohaddes-star/third-eye-kit</a>.</li>
          <li>Third Eye Kit — External References &amp; Media Coverage. Competition records, field-test documentation, and press coverage.</li>
          <li>Third Eye Kit — Bill of Materials. Component-level cost breakdown by node.</li>
          <li>TEKNOFEST Aerospace and Technology Festival, Barrier-Free Living category, Team ABRT01 (#431899).</li>
          <li>Field-test site: Herat Blind Support Centre, Herat, Afghanistan.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  `;
}

