    <div class="article-body">
      <p class="deck">Third Eye Kit v2 is a full engineering blueprint — a design and reference specification, not a finished or certified product — for a single wearable that lets a blind traveler feel the world in real time. It is the direct successor to the field-tested v1 prototype, built around a lesson v1's own field testing surfaced: a voice telling you where to go is too slow and never precise enough for a real road.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        ${iconSvg('warning-circle', 'fill')}
        <div>
          <strong>Status, read first</strong>
          <p>v1 is a tested prototype; v2, described here, is frozen at reference-spec level. The powered exoskeleton, hot-surface warning, and health features are novel and unproven, and must pass the staged safety validation below before anyone relies on them. Nothing here is medical advice, and the white cane and the wearer's own hearing remain primary — this system is designed to augment them, not replace them.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 id="tek2v1">Abstract</h2>
      <p>Almost every assistive-navigation product invests in "robot eyes" — a camera plus AI that describes the world back to the user in words. Third Eye Kit v2 is built around the observation that almost no one invests in the human input channel: how information actually gets into the person fast enough to act on. Its answer is a full-body haptic "buzz-language" paired with a gently-steering exoskeleton, both driven by direct physical sensing rather than AI inference, with an AI assistant kept strictly to a supporting role — it reads signs and describes scenes, but it never becomes the eyes and it never steers.</p>

      <h2 id="tek2v2">The market gap this fills</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>White cane</strong> — reliable ground contact, but misses everything above knee height and only signals "something's there," not what it is.</li>
        <li><strong>Guide dog</strong> — excellent, but roughly $50,000 and around two years of training; inaccessible to most.</li>
        <li><strong>Smart canes</strong> (e.g. WeWALK Smart Cane 2, ~$850–1,150) — add overhead-obstacle detection and a voice assistant, but are still fundamentally a cane for someone with strong cane skills.</li>
        <li><strong>AI glasses</strong> (Envision, Meta Ray-Ban-based, OrCam) — read text and describe scenes, but lack depth sensing and the sub-500&nbsp;ms latency safe walking needs, can mask traffic sounds, and must still be used with a cane.</li>
        <li><strong>Self-guiding robots and AI wearables</strong> (Glidance "Glide," biped.ai "NOA") — the most advanced tier, but Glide is a wheeled robot you push beside you, not worn, and most remain prototypes.</li>
        <li><strong>Apps and human services</strong> (BlindSquare, Seeing AI, Aira, Be My Eyes) — genuinely helpful, but can be wrong and must supplement the cane.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>None of the above is a road-safe, worn, all-in-one system, and none solves the input-channel problem of getting rich spatial information into the body faster than words. That is the space Third Eye Kit v2 is designed for.</p>

      <h2 id="tek2v3">From v1 to v2 — the pivot</h2>
      <p>v1 was four small microcontrollers, each owning a job in hard real time, so a slow task like sending a text could never freeze the fast task of ranging — ultrasonic sensing at head and shoe level, simple haptic and audible alerts, an LDR/LED light aid, and a GPS+GSM node that could text a family member the wearer's location, at a total build cost of about $112. It worked, and blind users were grateful. But testing with more advanced travelers surfaced the real lesson behind v2: people don't want to wait to be told where to go — they want to feel the world as it happens. That insight moved the project's center of gravity from "robot eyes" to the human input channel, which is why v2 centers on a full-body buzz-language and an exoskeleton rather than a smarter voice assistant.</p>

      <h2 id="tek2v4">Founding design rules</h2>
      <p>These are treated as hard constraints, not aspirations:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Life-critical sensing and steering is physics, not AI inference.</li>
        <li>The AI is an assistant only — it never becomes the eyes and never steers.</li>
        <li>Exoskeleton: warn, then gently steer, never lock.</li>
        <li>Fail transparent — on any fault or emergency stop, motors freewheel; a limb is never trapped.</li>
        <li>Real-time hardware isolation — the AI/cloud layer can never stall the safety layer.</li>
        <li>Augment, not replace, the cane and the wearer's hearing.</li>
        <li>Not a health monitor — the health features are for emergencies, not vitals or diagnosis.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="tek2v5">The buzz-language &amp; direct sensing</h2>
      <p>The input channel is a web of small vibration motors across the body. When something comes within about a meter, the motors nearest to it start a very gentle buzz that grows a little stronger as it gets closer — direction is which zone buzzes, distance is intensity and rhythm, and a distinct signature marks a special warning like heat or a drop-off. Motor placement is grounded in how skin actually feels: two-point discrimination is roughly 1–5&nbsp;mm on the fingertips but 40–70&nbsp;mm on the back and thighs, yet for vibration on the torso people can distinguish buzzers spaced below about 13&nbsp;mm — so motors pack closer where skin reads well (front torso, hands, wrists) and space out where it's coarse (back, thighs), giving a "feel where things are" effect with far fewer motors.</p>

      <p>No single sensor is trusted to carry the whole system; each covers the others' blind spots — multizone time-of-flight for walls, people, and drop-offs; downward sole time-of-flight for curbs and stairs; millimeter-wave radar for motion and glass, which cameras miss; a stereo depth camera; ultrasonic for close redundancy; and an IMU to stabilize all of it.</p>

      <div class="figure">
        <div class="figure-frame">
          <span class="fig-label">Fig. 01</span>
          ${iconSvg('map-trifold', 'regular', 'fig-ico')}
        </div>
        <div class="figure-cap">
          <span class="fc-num">Fig. 01</span>
          <span>Scanning gloves extend the same buzz-language to the hand as a "modern cane" for indoor and manual exploration — finding a doorway, a table edge, a handrail, or an empty seat — because indoors, GPS and city data are useless and the gloves, sole sensors, and body haptics carry the load instead.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 id="tek2v6">Four tiers of trust</h2>
      <p>The system is organized so that the more a layer is trusted with the person's life, the simpler and more deterministic it is: a <strong>Brain</strong> tier (Jetson-class compute, Linux) handles vision, scene AI, OCR, the assistant, and maps; a <strong>Spine</strong> tier arbitrates alerts and sequences haptic patterns; a <strong>Reflex</strong> tier does close-range ranging, buzz-language drive, and fault annunciation in hard real time (under 50&nbsp;ms); and an <strong>Exo-Motor</strong> tier runs gait-phase estimation and torque-capped assist. Above the isolation boundary sits everything best-effort; below it sits everything safety-critical.</p>

      <blockquote class="pull-quote">
        A stalled cloud call, a crashed AI model, or a hung map lookup above the line cannot freeze ranging, the buzz-language, or the motor loop below it.
        <cite>— Architecture &amp; the safety isolation boundary, §6</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <h2 id="tek2v7">The exoskeleton: warn → gently steer → never lock</h2>
      <p>A partial powered exoskeleton (hip, knee, and ankle assist) reduces walking fatigue and gently steers the wearer away from a confirmed hazard — always after a haptic warning, always as a small nudge the wearer can walk through, never a lock. It uses quasi-direct-drive actuators, which pair a high-torque motor with a low-ratio gear to stay backdrivable: easy to push, so a fault makes them freewheel rather than lock. Reference hip units run roughly 3.4&nbsp;kg with about 17.5&nbsp;Nm nominal torque and only about 0.4&nbsp;Nm of backdrive resistance, and comparable hip assist has been shown to cut walking effort by around 15%. The "never lock" rule is built into the hardware itself, not just the software: a separate high-voltage actuator rail is broken by a hardwired emergency-stop relay, independent of firmware.</p>

      <h2 id="tek2v8">Health, safety &amp; the staged validation gate</h2>
      <p>The exoskeleton, hot-surface warning, and health features are called out as novel and unproven in the project's own documentation — nobody has safely robot-steered a blind person's gait before. The design's own hazard analysis names failure modes like a false-positive steer, mid-stride power loss, actuator runaway, entrapment, and silent sensor failure, with mitigations including hard local torque bounds, a hardwired emergency stop, backdrivable freewheeling, and a fault-annunciation budget under 50&nbsp;ms so nothing fails silently.</p>
      <p>Before any powered steer reaches a real user, the spec requires clearing five gates in order:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>G0 — bench / hardware-in-the-loop.</strong> Every fault path resolves to zero-torque freewheel within budget; torque caps are hard.</li>
        <li><strong>G1 — able-bodied, blindfolded, harnessed.</strong> The steer is perceivable, overridable, and non-destabilizing.</li>
        <li><strong>G2 — supervised low-vision pilot</strong>, with fall protection and an orientation-and-mobility clinician present. Acceptable false-positive rate; cues correctly decoded; trust and cognitive load acceptable.</li>
        <li><strong>G3 — limited real-world use, cane retained.</strong> Safe behavior on real drop-offs, curbs, and glass; no unsafe surprises.</li>
        <li><strong>G4 — broader field use.</strong> Sustained safe use, where retention — whether people actually keep wearing it — is treated as the real success metric.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="tek2v9">Regulatory pathway</h2>
      <p>A powered wearable that physically assists and steers a person is very likely to be regulated as both a medical device and a physical assistant robot, with classification that is jurisdiction-specific and would need confirmation from regulatory professionals. The design is oriented from day one against ISO 13482 (personal-care and physical-assistant robots), ISO 14971 (medical-device risk management), IEC 60601 (medical electrical equipment), IEC 62304 (medical-device software), and ISO/TS 15066 (power-and-force-limiting concepts) — the same general path comparable powered exoskeletons like ReWalk and Ekso took to market, a multi-year, evidence-intensive process.</p>

      <h2 id="tek2v10">Honest limits &amp; open questions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Nobody has safely robot-steered a blind person's gait before — the exoskeleton is the novel, unproven, expensive piece.</li>
        <li>The effective-yet-safe steer torque is unknown until the G1–G2 gates actually measure it.</li>
        <li>Real-world false-positive rates are unknown until field testing produces them.</li>
        <li>Live traffic-signal data exists in only a few cities; a camera-read fallback carries most of the load elsewhere.</li>
        <li>Full-day runtime under real actuator loads must be measured, not assumed from a power budget.</li>
        <li>Long-term retention — whether people actually keep wearing it — is the ultimate test, and the reason the design includes a training game.</li>
        <li>The regulatory classification and clearance path are unconfirmed and will be slow.</li>
      </ul>

      <hr/>

      <p>v2's real departure from v1 isn't any single sensor upgrade — it's the decision to treat the human input channel, not the AI, as the hard problem, and to keep every life-critical decision on the physics side of a boundary the cloud can never stall. It inherits v1's context-bounded honesty directly: this document states plainly that the powered and health features are unbuilt and unproven, and names the exact gates that must be cleared, in order, before that changes. See <a href="/blog-detail?slug=third-eye-kit">Third Eye Kit (v1)</a> for the tested prototype this blueprint builds on.</p>

